.\" -*- mode: troff; coding: utf-8 -*-
.\" bidet3d(1) -- manual page for BIDeT3D
.TH BIDET3D 1 "2026-10-01" "BIDeT3D 0.1" "User Commands"
.if n .ad l
.nh
.SH NAME
bidet3d \- 3D WordArt for SIXEL terminals (BIDeT, in the third dimension)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
\fBbidet3d\fR [\fIoptions\fR] \fItext\fR ...
\fIcommand\fR | \fBbidet3d\fR [\fIoptions\fR]
\fBbidet3d\fR \fB\-\-list\-presets\fR
\fBbidet3d\fR \fB\-\-gallery\fR [\fItext\fR]
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
Use this after you're done with Toilet!
Why did I write this? Because I DEcided To. :)
.PP
.B bidet3d
renders text as 1990s\-style WordArt, extruded into real 3D, and prints it in
the terminal as SIXEL graphics.
It takes its text from the command line arguments, or from standard input if
there are none (or the argument is \fB\-\fR) and input is not a terminal.
With no text at all it draws "BIDeT3D".
Each line is warped onto a baseline, given a face (gradient, texture or solid
colour) and a lit bevel, extruded into a stack of depth slices, and drawn
through a perspective camera.
The picture is encoded as SIXEL and sent to the terminal, which must support
it (see
.BR "TERMINAL SUPPORT" ).
.PP
All 30 designs of
.I css3wordart
are provided as presets, including the arc, inverted\-arc and squeeze
baselines.
The picture can be rotated to any angle and can be animated.
.PP
It is the successor of BIDeT, which printed Type 1 PostScript fonts as flat
SIXEL pictures.
The common options keep their BIDeT meaning; see
.BR "DIFFERENCES FROM BIDET" .
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Text and appearance (shared with BIDeT)
.TP
.BR \-b ", " \-\-background =\fICOLOUR\fR
Background colour.
The default is to ask the terminal for its own colour, so that the picture
blends in; if the terminal will not say, a transparent background is used.
See
.BR "BACKGROUND COLOUR AND TRANSPARENCY" .
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-colour ", " \-\-color =\fICOLOUR\fR
Solid face colour.
This replaces the preset's gradient or texture, but keeps its shape, depth and
lighting.
Names, \fB#rrggbb\fR and other forms understood by Pillow's ImageColor are accepted.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-debug
Print timings, the backend in use, the raw replies the terminal gave to its
queries, and for animations the achieved frame rate, on standard error.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-font =\fIFILE\fR
Font file (\fB.ttf\fR, \fB.otf\fR, \fB.ttc\fR) or the name of a font file found
in the system font directories.
This overrides the preset's font.
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-line =\fIN\fR
Line spacing multiplier (default 1.0).
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-preserve
Keep the input's newlines instead of re\-wrapping the text to the wrap width.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-size =\fIPX\fR
Font size in pixels.
The default is to fit the picture to the terminal's size.
.TP
.BR \-w ", " \-\-width =\fIN\fR
Wrap width in characters (default 20).
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.SS Style and camera
.TP
.BR \-P ", " \-\-preset =\fINAME\fR
The WordArt style (default \fBrainbow\fR), or \fBrandom\fR.
See
.BR PRESETS .
.TP
.B \-\-list\-presets
List the preset names, one per line, and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-gallery
Draw every preset with the given text (default "BIDeT3D").
.TP
.BI \-\-depth= EM
Extrusion depth in em (default: per preset).
.TP
.BI \-\-view= YAW , PITCH , ROLL
All three camera angles in degrees (default: per preset).
Write it with an equals sign, as in \fB\-\-view=\-30,10,0\fR, so that a negative
first angle is not taken for an option.
.TP
.BI \-\-yaw= DEG
Just the yaw: turn left or right around the vertical axis.
.TP
.BI \-\-pitch= DEG
Just the pitch: tip back or forward.
.TP
.BI \-\-roll= DEG
Just the roll: clockwise rotation in the picture plane.
.TP
.BI \-\-shape= SHAPE
Baseline warp: \fBplain\fR, \fBarc\fR, \fBinverted\-arc\fR, \fBsqueeze\fR or
\fBwave\fR (default: per preset).
.TP
.BI \-\-perspective= F
Camera distance, in text widths (default 2.5).
Smaller values give stronger perspective.
.SS Animation
.TP
.B \-\-spin
Rotate the text around the vertical axis.
Ctrl\-C stops.
.TP
.BI \-\-sway= DEG
Swing \(+- \fIDEG\fR degrees around the chosen view instead of spinning.
This needs only half the frames of \fB\-\-spin\fR, so it starts faster.
.TP
.BI \-\-spin\-speed= DEG
Degrees per second (default 60).
.TP
.BI \-\-fps= N
Frames per second (default 10); this also sets the length of the loop.
.TP
.BI \-\-frames= N
Stop after showing \fIN\fR frames (default: until Ctrl\-C).
.TP
.B \-\-time\-machine
Cycle through the history of big terminal text, using your text: \fBbanner\fR(1)
(1983), \fBFIGlet\fR (1991), \fBTOIlet\fR (2004), \fBBIDeT\fR (2020) and finally
BIDeT3D, which then spins (or sways, with \fB\-\-sway\fR) until Ctrl\-C.
A year counter winds forward between the eras.
The 3D loop is rendered first, so the show itself runs smoothly.
It runs on the terminal's alternate screen, so your own screen is left as it was.
If \fBfiglet\fR and \fBtoilet\fR are installed, they are the ones used
(TOIlet with its \fBfuture\fR font and \fBgay\fR filter);
\fBbanner\fR(1) is used if present.
Otherwise imitations are drawn from the same text, which also look the part.
Set \fBBIDET3D_EMULATE\fR to always use the imitations.
\fB\-P\fR, \fB\-\-spin\-speed\fR, \fB\-\-fps\fR, \fB\-\-frames\fR and the camera options apply
to the final stage.
Cannot be combined with \fB\-\-png\fR or \fB\-\-gallery\fR.
.TP
.BI \-\-stage\-time= SEC
Seconds to show each of the earlier eras with \fB\-\-time\-machine\fR (default 3).
.PP
The whole loop is rendered first (a progress counter is shown), every frame
is quantized to one shared palette and encoded once, and the cached SIXEL is
then replayed on a steady clock.
How smooth it looks therefore depends on how quickly the terminal swallows
SIXEL, not on how fast the script renders.
If the terminal cannot keep up, frames are skipped instead of slowing the
rotation.
If it stutters, try \fB\-\-fps\fR 6..15, \fB\-\-colors\fR 64, a smaller
\fB\-s\fR or \fB\-\-max\-width\fR, and use \fB\-d\fR to see the timings.
Frames are wrapped in synchronized\-output sequences (mode 2026) where the
terminal supports them.
.SS Output
.TP
.BI \-\-colors= N
SIXEL palette size (default 256).
Fewer colours mean smaller output and more banding.
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-width= PX
Cap the width of the picture.
Smaller pictures render faster and send less data.
.TP
.BI \-\-ss= N
Supersampling factor (default 2; 1 when animating).
.TP
.B \-\-dither
Floyd\-Steinberg dithering for still pictures.
The default is off: the palette is built for the picture, and dithering only
adds speckle.
.TP
.B \-\-transparent
Leave the background unpainted so that the terminal shows its own.
See
.BR "BACKGROUND COLOUR AND TRANSPARENCY" .
.TP
.BI \-\-png= FILE
Write a PNG file instead of printing SIXEL.
The terminal is not queried.
.TP
.B \-\-force
Print SIXEL even if the terminal does not report SIXEL support.
Setting \fBLSIX_FORCE_SIXEL_SUPPORT\fR has the same effect.
.TP
.BI \-\-cell= WxH
Size of a terminal character cell in pixels, for terminals where it cannot be
detected (see also \fBBIDET3D_CELL\fR).
.TP
.BI \-\-texture\-dir= DIR
Directory containing the Word textures; see
.BR TEXTURES .
.SH PRESETS
The presets are, in order:
.PP
.nf
  outline         up              arc             squeeze
  inverted\-arc    basic\-stack     italic\-outline  slate
  mauve           graydient       red\-blue        brown\-stack
  radial          purple          green\-marble    rainbow
  aqua            texture\-stack   paper\-bag       sunset
  tilt            blues           yellow\-dash     green\-stack
  chrome          marble\-slab     gray\-block      superhero
  horizon         stack\-3d
.fi
.PP
Presets such as \fBup\fR, \fBarc\fR and \fBsqueeze\fR are drawn in black "ink"
which, being designed for a light page, is shown in white on a dark terminal.
.SH TERMINAL SUPPORT
The terminal must be able to display SIXEL graphics.
Known to work: mintty (Cygwin), xterm (started with \fB\-ti vt340\fR), mlterm
and Windows Terminal 1.22 or later.
Also reported to support SIXEL: foot, iTerm2 and WezTerm (but not WezTerm on
Windows with ConPTY).
A longer list is in
.I TERMINAL\-SUPPORT\-LIST.txt
in the BIDeT source tree.
.PP
Before drawing anything,
.B bidet3d
asks the terminal whether it reports SIXEL support (the DA1 attribute 4) and
stops with "Sixel not supported" if it says it does not.
.B \-\-force
overrides this.
Output that is piped, and \fB\-\-png\fR, skip the check.
.SH BACKGROUND
In 1983 AT&T released
.BR banner (1)
with UNIX System V.
It allowed you to create a banner of text made out of ASCII characters.
.PP
In 1987 DEC released the VT300 series of computer terminals.
These came with the ability to display graphics via the new SIXEL encoding
format.
.PP
In 1991 Chappell and Chai released
.BR FIGlet (6)
as an extension of
.BR banner (1)
that had numerous "fonts."
.PP
In 2004 Sam Hocevar released
.BR TOIlet (1)
as an extension of
.BR FIGlet (6).
It has more fonts, colour and other neat features.
.PP
In 2014 Hayaki Saito wrote the
.BR img2sixel (1)
utility, part of libsixel.
.PP
In 2016 Hayaki Saito added SIXEL graphics support to
.BR mintty (1).
.PP
In 2020 Chris Pollitt released BIDeT as an alternative to
.BR TOIlet (1).
It makes use of SIXEL graphics to display Type 1 PostScript fonts.
Sam Hocevar, to whom the author wrote about it, replied that he liked it, and
that projects like it gave him the incentive to finally improve
.BR TOIlet (1)
with 256\-colour terminal features and more Unicode and emoji support.
.PP
In 2025 BIDeT was ported from Perl to Python (\fBbidet2.py\fR).
Neither version is maintained any longer.
.PP
In 2026, with SIXEL by then also available in Windows Terminal and many other
terminals, Chris Pollitt added the third dimension: BIDeT3D.
It takes the WordArt designs of
.IR css3wordart ,
which approximated them with CSS and called the arc, inverted\-arc and squeeze
baselines "not achievable", and renders them for real, as a small software
renderer in Python (Pillow and numpy), encoding the result with libsixel.
.SH "BACKGROUND COLOUR AND TRANSPARENCY"
In the same exchange in which it asks about SIXEL support,
.B bidet3d
asks the terminal for its background colour (OSC 11), so that the picture
blends in.
That colour is kept exact through quantization, within the 1% steps that SIXEL
colour registers allow (libsixel's own quantizer, for example, turns white
into 247).
\fB\-b\fR \fICOLOUR\fR overrides it.
.PP
If the terminal will not say (Windows Terminal does not answer OSC 11), a still
picture is sent with a transparent background, so that the terminal shows its
own colour.
\fB\-\-transparent\fR forces this.
Transparent pictures keep a real alpha channel, so edge colours are the
material's own and there is no halo of the wrong background; the edge itself
is a hard 1\-bit step, which is all SIXEL transparency can express.
.PP
An animation on such a terminal erases its picture area before drawing each
frame, since otherwise the previous frame would show through the unpainted
pixels.
This may flicker a little on terminals without synchronized output;
\fB\-b\fR \fICOLOUR\fR gives opaque frames instead.
.SH TEXTURES
Four presets (\fBgreen\-marble\fR, \fBmarble\-slab\fR, \fBpaper\-bag\fR and
\fBtexture\-stack\fR) use the Word textures from css3wordart.
That repository has no license file, so they are not bundled.
Run
.B get\-textures.sh
to fetch them into the \fBtextures\fR directory next to the program, or point
\fB\-\-texture\-dir\fR or \fBBIDET3D_TEXTURES\fR at a css3wordart checkout.
Without them a procedural stand\-in is used, and everything still works.
.SH REQUIREMENTS
.IP \(bu 4
Python 3 with Pillow and numpy.
It also runs with old versions (tested with Python 3.7, Pillow 5.4 and
numpy 1.16), though the animations are slow on small machines.
.IP \(bu 4
libsixel: either its Python binding (\fBpip install libsixel\-python\fR), which
is used when present, or the
.BR img2sixel (1)
program.
.IP \(bu 4
A terminal that handles SIXEL graphics.
.IP \(bu 4
Fonts: Arial Bold, Times New Roman Bold and Impact are used when found
(Windows, Cygwin, WSL, macOS); otherwise Liberation or DejaVu.
\fB\-f\fR selects any font file.
.PP
Unlike BIDeT, neither Perl, Ghostscript nor netpbm is needed.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B BIDET3D_TEXTURES
Directory searched for the Word textures.
.TP
.B BIDET3D_CELL
Terminal cell size in pixels as \fIWxH\fR, like \fB\-\-cell\fR.
.TP
.B BIDET3D_EMULATE
If set, \fB\-\-time\-machine\fR draws its own imitations of banner, FIGlet and TOIlet
even if the real programs are installed.
.TP
.B BIDET3D_SERIAL
If set, render animation frames in one process instead of several.
.TP
.B LSIX_FORCE_SIXEL_SUPPORT
If set, print SIXEL even if the terminal does not report support (see
\fB\-\-force\fR).
.TP
.B TERM
Used to detect terminals with special limits: xterm cannot show SIXEL wider
than 1000 pixels, and yaft cannot answer the SIXEL support query, so it is
assumed to support SIXEL.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I bidet3d.py
The program.
.TP
.I textures/
Optional Word textures (see
.BR TEXTURES ).
Also searched: \fI/usr/local/share/BIDeT3D/textures\fR.
.TP
.I get\-textures.sh
Fetches the textures.
.TP
.I test.sh
Smoke test: renders every preset to PNG, and to SIXEL if \fBimg2sixel\fR is
present.
.TP
.I 3RDPARTY
What this borrows from, and from whom.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
bidet3d \-P superhero "Hello, World!"
bidet3d \-P chrome \-\-spin "Spin me"
bidet3d \-\-sway 25 \-\-fps 8 \-\-max\-width 640 "Swaying"
bidet3d \-P arc \-c gold \-\-yaw=\-20 "Gold arc"
bidet3d \-P random \-b black "Surprise"
bidet3d \-\-gallery
bidet3d \-\-time\-machine "BIDeT"
bidet3d \-\-time\-machine \-\-stage\-time 5 \-P superhero \-\-sway 25 "Hello"
echo "From a pipe" | bidet3d \-P rainbow
bidet3d \-P chrome \-\-png chrome.png "Look, a PNG"
.fi
.SH "DIFFERENCES FROM BIDET"
.IP \(bu 4
Fonts are TrueType/OpenType files, not Type 1 PostScript; there is no
\fB\-f list\fR or \fB\-c list\fR.
Use \fB\-\-list\-presets\fR and \fB\-P random\fR.
.IP \(bu 4
\fB\-c\fR sets a solid face colour only if given; by default the preset's
own material is used.
.IP \(bu 4
The BIDeT options \fB\-\-ansi\fR and \fB\-\-rotate\fR are not provided;
rotation is done with \fB\-\-yaw\fR, \fB\-\-pitch\fR and \fB\-\-roll\fR.
.IP \(bu 4
\fB\-b\fR defaults to the terminal's own background (or transparent), and
\fB\-s\fR to a size that fits the terminal.
.IP \(bu 4
\fB\-d\fR prints debugging information.
.SH BUGS
.IP \(bu 4
Near edge\-on views (yaw about 90 degrees) the extruded side walls show a
little banding.
Raise \fBSLICE_DENSITY\fR in the script if it bothers you.
.IP \(bu 4
SIXEL colour registers have 1% steps, so a colour can be off by one level.
.IP \(bu 4
Starting an animation can take a while on a slow machine: the loop is
rendered before the first frame is shown.
.IP \(bu 4
Frame erasing on terminals that do not report their background has not been
verified on every terminal.
